      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Sacrificial anti-graffiti coatings work by providing a wax or silicone-based barrier layer that is removed along with the graffiti paint during pressure washing. Because the coating itself is consumed in each cleaning cycle, the surface must be recoated after each graffiti removal event. Permanent anti-graffiti coatings, by contrast, are chemically inert, ultra-low-surface-energy films (typically polyurethane, fluoropolymer, or silicone-polyurethane hybrid systems) that prevent graffiti from forming strong adhesive bonds to the substrate. The graffiti can be wiped or solvent-cleaned off the coating's surface repeatedly over a service life of 5–15 years without removing or degrading the coating itself. Sacrificial systems are lower-cost and better for heritage masonry where the substrate cannot tolerate solvents; permanent systems are preferred for high-traffic infrastructure where frequent recoating is impractical."
      }
