      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do permanent anti-graffiti coatings prevent graffiti from bonding to surfaces?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Permanent anti-graffiti coatings function by reducing the surface energy of the substrate to below the surface tension of common spray paints and markers, preventing wetting and adhesive bond formation. Most spray paints have a surface tension of approximately 28–35 mN/m. A fluoropolymer or silicone-modified polyurethane coating with a surface energy of 15–22 mN/m cannot be effectively wetted by the graffiti paint — the paint forms discrete droplets (high contact angle) rather than spreading into a continuous film. Without adequate wetting, only weak van der Waals interactions develop at the graffiti-coating interface, and the graffiti film can be removed by solvent wiping or pressure washing with minimal mechanical effort. The coating remains intact on the substrate after cleaning."
      }
