      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are anti-graffiti coatings safe for heritage and historic masonry buildings?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Heritage masonry presents special challenges for anti-graffiti coating selection. The primary concern is reversibility: any coating applied to a listed or historic building should be removable without damaging the original substrate. Sacrificial wax-based anti-graffiti coatings are generally preferred for heritage masonry because they are vapour-permeable (allowing the wall to breathe), removable with mild solvents, and do not alter the visual appearance of the stone or brick significantly. Solvent-borne permanent anti-graffiti coatings with fluoropolymer or two-pack polyurethane chemistry should generally be avoided on unpainted historic masonry as they alter the substrate appearance, reduce breathability, and are extremely difficult to remove without mechanical abrasion. Water-based silane-siloxane penetrating hydrophobic treatments are sometimes used as a compromise — they provide good graffiti resistance without forming a surface film."
      }
