        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The service life of a permanent anti-graffiti coating depends primarily on the binder chemistry, UV exposure level, substrate movement, and cleaning frequency. High-quality two-pack aliphatic polyurethane anti-graffiti topcoats typically provide 8–12 years of service on sheltered substrates and 5–8 years on directly exposed facades before the surface energy rises back above the graffiti threshold due to UV degradation and weathering. Fluoropolymer PVDF-based systems are the most durable, retaining low surface energy for 15–20 years even in direct weathering. Each graffiti cleaning cycle with aggressive solvents gradually degrades the coating's surface energy — a 5-clean-cycle accelerated test is standard during product qualification to confirm that anti-graffiti performance is maintained through the expected service cleaning cycles."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What surface preparation is required before applying an anti-graffiti coating?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Surface preparation is the single most critical factor in anti-graffiti coating performance. All surfaces must be clean, dry, and free of existing graffiti, oil contamination, form-release agents, efflorescence (on masonry), and any previous coating that is not fully adhered or compatible with the anti-graffiti system. On masonry substrates, light abrasive blasting or high-pressure washing (minimum 2,500 psi) removes surface laitance and opens the substrate pores for penetrating primers. On metal and gloss surfaces, mechanical abrasion to at least Sa 2.5 profile (or equivalent) is required to ensure adhesion. For two-pack polyurethane anti-graffiti systems applied over existing paint, the existing paint must pass a cross-cut adhesion test (ISO 2409, Grade 0–1) and be chemically compatible. Application must occur when the substrate is above 3°C above the dew point to prevent moisture condensation, which dramatically reduces adhesion."
