          width="900" 
          height="500"
          style="width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-muted); text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        Figure 1: Water droplet contact angle on a permanent anti-graffiti fluoropolymer coating — the high contact angle (>100°) demonstrates the ultra-low surface energy that prevents graffiti media from wetting and bonding to the protected surface.
      </p>

      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-4">
        <h2>4. The Science of Non-Stick Surfaces: Contact Angle &amp; Surface Energy</h2>
        <p>
          The performance of any anti-graffiti coating is fundamentally governed by its surface energy — the thermodynamic work required to create a unit area of new surface. Surface energy determines whether a liquid (graffiti paint) will spread on a solid (the coating) or bead up and refuse to wet it. The quantitative measure of this wetting behaviour is the contact angle (θ), measured through the liquid for a sessile droplet resting on a flat surface. As described in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fluoropolymer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); text-decoration: underline;">Wikipedia article on fluoropolymers</a>, the extremely low surface energy of fluorinated polymers (15–18 mN/m for PTFE; 18–22 mN/m for PVDF) arises directly from the electronegativity and non-polarizability of the fluorine atoms, which minimise London dispersion forces and dipole interactions at the surface.
        </p>
        <p>
          Young's equation governs the relationship between contact angle and the three interfacial energies at the three-phase contact line: γSV (solid-vapour) = γSL (solid-liquid) + γLV (liquid-vapour) · cos θ. When the solid surface energy γSV is very low (fluoropolymer, silicone), the contact angle θ is large (>90° for water, >70° for typical spray paint solvents), indicating poor wetting. At contact angles above approximately 70° for a liquid with the surface tension of typical graffiti spray paint (28–33 mN/m), the work of adhesion at the paint-coating interface is insufficient to develop a continuous, strongly bonded film. The graffiti film remains in a "weakly bonded" state — mechanically continuous but lacking the adhesive energy to resist a pressure wash or solvent wipe.
        </p>

        <div class="box" style="margin: 30px 0; background: var(--navy-dark); color: #ffffff; border-left: 5px solid var(--gold); padding: 24px; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0; font-family: 'Source Serif 4', serif;">
          <strong style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; color: var(--gold); font-size: 1.15rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">Critical Surface Energy Threshold for Anti-Graffiti Performance</strong>
          <span style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6;">
            For reliable anti-graffiti performance against common aerosol spray paints, a coating surface energy below 22 mN/m is the industry target. Standard acrylic 2K polyurethane coatings without fluorinated additives have surface energies of 35–42 mN/m — insufficient for anti-graffiti use. Addition of 2–5% fluorinated acrylic co-resin or PTFE micropowder to a PU topcoat typically reduces surface energy to 18–22 mN/m. Pure PVDF and PTFE coatings achieve 15–18 mN/m. Surface energy is measured by sessile drop contact angle goniometry using test liquids of known surface tension (water, diiodomethane) and applying the Owens-Wendt method to resolve polar and dispersive components separately.
          </span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
