      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-6">
        <h2>6. Substrate Preparation, Application &amp; Maintenance</h2>
        <p>
          The performance of any anti-graffiti coating — sacrificial or permanent — is ultimately determined not by the coating chemistry alone, but by the quality of surface preparation and application conditions. Anti-graffiti coatings are applied at dry film thicknesses of 25–75 µm (permanent 2K PU) to 15–30 µm (sacrificial wax), meaning that any surface contamination, moisture, or adhesion failure in the primer system is amplified across the coating's entire service life.
        </p>

        <h3 style="font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px;">A. Surface Preparation by Substrate Type</h3>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px; line-height: 1.9; margin-bottom: 20px;">
          <li><strong>Concrete &amp; Masonry:</strong> High-pressure water jet cleaning (2,500–3,000 psi) to remove surface laitance, dirt, moss, and efflorescence. Allow minimum 48 hours drying time at 15°C+ before coating. Fill voids and cracks with compatible repair mortar. Apply penetrating silane primer for permanent 2K PU systems to seal porosity and improve adhesion. For sacrificial wax, primer is generally not required on clean bare masonry.</li>
          <li><strong>Steel &amp; Metal:</strong> Abrasive blast to minimum Sa 2.5 (near-white metal) profile per ISO 8501-1 for permanent anti-graffiti systems over bare metal. Apply epoxy primer (50–75 µm DFT) followed by intermediate epoxy mastic coat before anti-graffiti topcoat. The anti-graffiti topcoat must be applied within the recoat window of the intermediate coat (typically 6–24 hours) to ensure intercoat adhesion. Contamination with oil or grease after blasting — even fingerprints — must be avoided entirely.</li>
          <li><strong>Previously Painted Surfaces:</strong> The existing coating must pass ISO 2409 cross-cut adhesion test (Grade 0–1) and be chemically compatible with the anti-graffiti topcoat binder. Chalky or glossy existing surfaces require light abrasion sanding (180-grit) and solvent wipe before topcoat application. Test adhesion of the anti-graffiti system to the existing paint on a small area using pull-off adhesion testing (ASTM D4541) — minimum 1.5 MPa before proceeding to full application.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px;">B. Application Conditions &amp; Maintenance Protocol</h3>
        <p>
          Two-pack polyurethane anti-graffiti topcoats must be applied with the substrate temperature at least 3°C above the dew point — moisture condensation on the substrate or in the coating film causes adhesion failure, blistering, and complete coating breakdown. Relative humidity must be below 85% during application and the initial cure period (minimum 4 hours). Pot life after mixing is typically 2–4 hours at 20°C — work in small batches and discard any material showing increased viscosity or gelation. Airless spray (tip size 0.013–0.017") or conventional air spray at 2.5–3.5 bar provides the smoothest finish and most uniform DFT. Brush or roller application produces acceptable results for small areas but may show texture that reduces anti-graffiti surface uniformity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Maintenance of permanent anti-graffiti coatings requires periodic inspection (annually) to check for mechanical damage, coating holidays, and surface energy degradation using contact angle measurement. Early-stage graffiti that is removed promptly (within 24–48 hours of application) is significantly easier to clean than aged, hardened graffiti — UV light accelerates the crosslinking and solvent evaporation of aerosol spray paints, increasing their bond strength over time. A documented maintenance protocol specifying the approved removal solvents, cleaning procedure, and inspection criteria is an essential deliverable for any anti-graffiti coating installation on high-value infrastructure assets.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the difference between sacrificial and permanent anti-graffiti coatings?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Sacrificial coatings are consumed during cleaning — the wax or silicone film is stripped along with the graffiti by hot pressure washing, and the surface must be recoated afterwards. Permanent coatings are chemically inert, ultra-low-surface-energy films (aliphatic PU, fluoropolymer) that prevent graffiti from bonding strongly, allowing it to be wiped or solvent-cleaned off repeatedly without removing or degrading the coating — service life 5–15+ years. Sacrificial systems are preferred for heritage masonry; permanent for high-traffic infrastructure.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. How do permanent anti-graffiti coatings prevent graffiti from bonding?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              By reducing the coating's surface energy below the surface tension of common spray paints (~28–33 mN/m). When the coating's surface energy is below ~22 mN/m (fluoropolymer / PTFE-modified PU), the graffiti paint cannot wet the surface adequately — it forms high contact-angle droplets rather than a continuous film. Without proper wetting, only weak van der Waals adhesion develops at the graffiti-coating interface, and the graffiti film is easily removed by solvent wiping or pressure washing with minimal mechanical effort.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What solvents remove graffiti from permanent anti-graffiti coated surfaces?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Isopropyl alcohol (IPA), ethanol, and proprietary graffiti removers based on citrus-terpene (d-limonene) or dipropylene glycol monomethyl ether are effective on most aerosol spray paints against aliphatic PU anti-graffiti coatings. Acetone can be used cautiously but should be tested on a small area first — some PU topcoats are acetone-sensitive. Fluoropolymer coatings tolerate a broader range of solvents due to their inherent chemical inertness. Alkaline hot water pressure wash (2,000–3,000 psi, 70–80°C) is the approved solvent-free method for VOC-restricted sites.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Are anti-graffiti coatings safe for heritage and historic masonry buildings?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Sacrificial wax and silicone wax coatings are generally acceptable for heritage masonry because they are vapour-permeable, visually unobtrusive, and fully reversible — removed during standard hot water cleaning without substrate damage. Permanent film-forming coatings (2K PU, fluoropolymer) are generally not approved for listed or historic masonry as they alter appearance, reduce breathability, and cannot be removed without abrasive blasting that damages original stonework. Penetrating silane-siloxane hydrophobic treatments offer a compromise with good graffiti resistance and no visible surface film.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. How long do permanent anti-graffiti coatings last?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              High-quality aliphatic 2K polyurethane anti-graffiti topcoats typically provide 8–12 years on sheltered substrates and 5–8 years on directly UV-exposed facades before surface energy rises above the graffiti-resistance threshold. PVDF and FEVE fluoropolymer systems retain performance for 15–20 years even in direct weathering. Aggressive solvent cleaning accelerates surface energy degradation — a 5-cleaning-cycle accelerated test is standard in product qualification to confirm anti-graffiti performance is maintained through the expected service life.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What surface preparation is required before applying an anti-graffiti coating?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              All surfaces must be clean, dry, free of contamination, and — for metal — abrasive-blasted to minimum Sa 2.5 with a compatible primer system. Masonry must be high-pressure washed and allowed 48+ hours to dry. Existing paint must pass ISO 2409 cross-cut adhesion (Grade 0–1) and be chemically compatible. Application must proceed only when the substrate temperature is at least 3°C above the dew point, relative humidity is below 85%, and ambient temperature is within the coating manufacturer's recommended range. Surface preparation accounts for the majority of anti-graffiti coating field failures — it should receive the same rigour as the coating specification itself.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

